### Q: "Tell me about a time you delivered something complex under tight deadlines."

> **Situation:** "We had a hard business deadline for the Product Feature Store go-live — the forecasting engine needed our features by a specific date to calibrate for a seasonal peak."
>
> **Task:** "I had to deliver end-to-end: Bronze through Gold, 12 feature tables, point-in-time joins, full UAT sign-off, and production deployment — in 6 weeks."
>
> **Action:** "I broke it into two-week sprints with clear milestones. Sprint 1: Bronze landing + schema contracts. Sprint 2: Silver CDC + SCD2 + quality gates. Sprint 3: Gold feature tables + PIT joins + UAT + deployment. I assigned domain ownership — one engineer per feature domain — and ran daily standups to unblock issues fast. I also negotiated with the business to reduce scope from 15 to 12 feature tables by deferring three that weren't critical for the initial calibration."
>
> **Result:** "Delivered on time. All 12 feature tables passed UAT validation. The forecasting engine achieved a 14% accuracy improvement compared to the previous feature set. The remaining 3 tables were delivered in the next sprint."

---

### Q: "Tell me about a time you had to push back on a request."

> **Situation:** "A product manager wanted us to expose raw Bronze data directly to their analysts via a SQL endpoint — they wanted speed and didn't want to wait for Silver/Gold processing."
>
> **Task:** "I needed to say no without being obstructive, and offer a viable alternative."
>
> **Action:** "I explained the risks: Bronze data has duplicates, schema inconsistencies, no quality validation. If they built dashboards on it, they'd get incorrect numbers and lose trust in the platform. Instead, I offered a 'fast Silver' layer — a lightweight cleansed view with just deduplication and null handling, updated within 30 minutes of Bronze landing. Not full SCD2, just clean enough for directional analytics."
>
> **Result:** "They accepted the compromise. The fast Silver view was ready in 3 days. They got their speed, we maintained data quality standards, and it became a standard pattern we reuse for other 'exploration-grade' use cases."

---

### Q: "Tell me about a time you mentored someone."

> **Situation:** "A junior engineer joined our managed service partner team. Good Python skills, but no experience with Delta Lake, Databricks, or production-grade data engineering patterns."
>
> **Task:** "Get them productive within 3 weeks without slowing down the team's delivery."
>
> **Action:** "I created a structured onboarding path. Week 1: documentation reading + environment setup + observe code reviews. Week 2: paired programming on a small BAU ticket — I drove first, then they drove while I watched. Week 3: solo ticket with review. I also recorded a 20-minute walkthrough of our architecture that any future joiner could watch.
>
> Beyond technical skills, I taught them our engineering culture — why we enforce tests, why we document decisions, why PRs need context in the description. These habits matter more than knowing a specific API."
>
> **Result:** "Within a month they were independently delivering BAU tickets. Within three months they were contributing to feature development. They told me later it was the most structured onboarding they'd experienced."

